-- Security: guessing PINs, and why most intrusion alarms are false
--
-- Two questions that security teams answer with probabilities: how much a limit on wrong PIN
-- guesses protects a stolen card or phone, and how much an intrusion-detection alarm should be
-- trusted.

-- 1. PIN guessing and lockout ---------------------------------------------------------
-- A thief with a stolen card or phone tries the most popular PINs first. How likely the thief
-- is to succeed depends on how people choose PINs and on how many guesses the system allows.
--
-- Nick Berry of DataGenetics analysed 3.4 million four-digit sequences from leaked password
-- databases ("PIN number analysis", 2012). The 20 most common, which together make up 26.83%,
-- are below. `pin` is the rank of the victim's PIN on that list, and 0 means it isn't on it.
input pin : Num ~ {1: 0.10713, 2: 0.06016, 3: 0.01881, 4: 0.01197, 5: 0.00745,
                   6: 0.00616, 7: 0.00613, 8: 0.00526, 9: 0.00516, 10: 0.00512,
                   11: 0.00451, 12: 0.00419, 13: 0.00395, 14: 0.00391, 15: 0.00366,
                   16: 0.00304, 17: 0.00303, 18: 0.00293, 19: 0.00290, 20: 0.00285,
                   0: 0.73168}

-- The thief guesses in order of popularity, and the device locks (a card) or erases itself (an
-- iPhone with "Erase Data" turned on) after `limit` wrong guesses.
system Thief(limit : Num) {
  var guesses : Num := 0
  var unlocked : Bool := false
  var locked : Bool := false
  step {
    if not unlocked and not locked {
      guesses := guesses + 1
      if pin == guesses { unlocked := true }
      else if guesses >= limit { locked := true }
    }
  }
}

-- The lockout holds however long the thief keeps trying.
theorem card_lockout : Thief(3) |= always guesses <= 3
theorem phone_lockout : Thief(10) |= always guesses <= 10

-- Banks lock a card after 3 wrong guesses; an iPhone with "Erase Data" erases itself after 10
-- (Apple Platform Security guide). With PINs chosen like these, both limits still let the thief
-- in surprisingly often.
claim card_three_guesses : Thief(3) |= eventually unlocked        -- 18.61%
claim phone_ten_guesses : Thief(10) |= eventually unlocked        -- 23.335%

-- Sequences from leaked passwords are only a stand-in for real PINs. Bonneau, Preibusch and
-- Anderson ("A birthday present every eleven wallets?", Financial Cryptography 2012) surveyed
-- how people actually choose banking PINs. They estimate that a thief with 6 guesses (3 at a
-- cash machine and 3 at a shop terminal) succeeds 1.94% of the time, and only 0.24% if the bank
-- refuses the most popular PINs. But a stolen wallet usually holds an ID with a birth date,
-- and many PINs are built from it: with the birth date, 6 guesses succeed 8.23% of the time,
-- and still 5.63% with the blocklist. Whether the bank uses a blocklist is a fuzzy parameter,
-- and the question is what happens to a batch of 20 stolen wallets.
param success_6 : Num ~= {0.0823: 1, 0.0563: 1}     -- without and with a blocklist
input cracked : Num ~ binomial(20, success_6)
#expect cracked
#prob cracked >= 1

-- 2. Intrusion-detection alarms -------------------------------------------------------
-- Stefan Axelsson ("The base-rate fallacy and the difficulty of intrusion detection", ACM
-- TISSEC 2000) works through a site that logs 1,000,000 audit records a day and sees 2
-- intrusions a day, each spanning about 10 records. Grouped into events of 10 records, that is
-- 2 intrusions among 100,000 events, a base rate of 2 in 100,000. His point is that even a very
-- accurate detector produces mostly false alarms at that base rate.
input intrusion : Bool ~ flip(2 / 100000)
-- the detector catches 70% or 100% of intrusions, and raises a false alarm on 1 in 100,000 or
-- 1 in 1,000 normal events; these are the cases Axelsson works through
param detection : Num ~= {0.7: 1, 1: 1}
param false_alarm : Num ~= {0.00001: 1, 0.001: 1}
input alarm : Bool ~ if intrusion then flip(detection) else flip(false_alarm)

-- How likely an alarm is to be a real intrusion: at best 2/3, with a perfect detector and one
-- false alarm per 100,000 events, and only about 2% at one per 1,000.
#prob intrusion given alarm
-- How many alarms a day the analysts see.
#expect (if alarm then 100000 else 0)
-- Axelsson notes that operators start ignoring a detector whose alarms are mostly false. For
-- at least half of all alarms to be real, the false alarm rate has to be the lower one.
claim mostly_real : intrusion given alarm with prob >= 0.5
